{"id":"ate-la-abaixo-2010","title":"Até Lá Abaixo: Lisbon to the 2010 World Cup","who":"Tiago Carrasco, João Henriques and João Fontes","when":"2010, about 150 days","vehicle":"4x4 jeep","kind":"4x4","color":"#b91c1c","media":[{"kind":"book","title":"Até Lá Abaixo","by":"Tiago Carrasco"},{"kind":"film","title":"Até Lá Abaixo: O Outro Lado do Futebol em África","by":"João Fontes","note":"Documentary, premiered at the Offside Lisboa festival"}],"summary":"A journalist, a photographer and a cameraman quit their jobs to drive a jeep from Portugal to South Africa for the first World Cup played in Africa: about 30,000 km through 21 African countries in five months. Football as a common language from Morocco to Johannesburg, and a journey about fear and setbacks too; in Zimbabwe a gang of street kids slashed a tyre and tried to steal their gear. It became a book (2011) and a documentary.","sources":[{"label":"Observador: Cinco meses num jipe para ver jogos de futebol? (2018)","url":"https://observador.pt/2018/05/25/cinco-meses-num-jipe-para-ver-jogos-de-futebol-isso-e-muito-mais/"},{"label":"Bertrand: Até Lá Abaixo (book)","url":"https://www.bertrand.pt/livro/ate-la-abaixo-tiago-carrasco/10918180"},{"label":"Ludopédio: the documentary","url":"https://ludopedio.org.br/biblioteca/ate-la-abaixo-filme/"}],"approximate":"Only the countries named in the sources (21 were crossed); the order between them and the roads are approximate, and the long stretch from The Gambia to Angola is not drawn.","km":12577,"bbox":[-16.9609,-26.2046,31.0618,38.7103],"stops":[{"name":"Lisboa","place":"Lisboa, Portugal","at":[38.7078,-9.1366],"date":"2010","text":"Three friends leave their jobs and set off south in a jeep.","photo":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Lisboa_-_Portugal_%2852597836992%29.jpg/500px-Lisboa_-_Portugal_%2852597836992%29.jpg","page":"Lisbon","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lisboa_-_Portugal_%2852597836992%29.jpg","credit":"Vitor Oliveira from Torres Vedras, PORTUGAL","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0"}},{"name":"Tarifa","place":"Tarifa, Spain","at":[36.0129,-5.605],"text":"Through Spain to the Strait of Gibraltar."},{"name":"Tangier","place":"Tangier, Morocco","at":[35.7695,-5.8045],"text":"The ferry over the strait into Africa.","by":"ferry","photo":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Tanger_cor.jpg/500px-Tanger_cor.jpg","page":"Tangier","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tanger_cor.jpg","credit":"Ifni95","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"}},{"name":"Morocco","place":"Marrakech, Morocco","at":[31.6258,-7.9892],"text":"South through Morocco, the first of 21 African countries.","photo":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Marrakech%2C_Morocco_%285421595453%29_%285%29.jpg/500px-Marrakech%2C_Morocco_%285421595453%29_%285%29.jpg","page":"Marrakesh","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marrakech%2C_Morocco_%285421595453%29_%285%29.jpg","credit":"YoTuT from United States","license":"CC BY 2.0"}},{"name":"The Gambia","place":"Banjul, Gambia","at":[13.4554,-16.5756],"text":"Down the West African coast.","photo":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Gambia%2C_Banjul%2C_Arch_22.jpg/500px-Gambia%2C_Banjul%2C_Arch_22.jpg","page":"Banjul","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gambia%2C_Banjul%2C_Arch_22.jpg","credit":"Hannu Sääskilahti","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"}},{"name":"Angola","place":"Luanda, Angola","at":[-8.8273,13.244],"text":"Portuguese-speaking Angola, after West and Central Africa.","by":"unknown","photo":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Luanda_2025_focused_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Luanda_2025_focused_%28cropped%29.jpg","page":"Luanda","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luanda_2025_focused_%28cropped%29.jpg","credit":"Iamdelcioborges","license":"CC BY 4.0"}},{"name":"Zambia","place":"Lusaka, Zambia","at":[-15.4163,28.2818],"text":"East acr